  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Monday...\n\nA persistent ridge over the western US will continue to bring\nwarm temperatures as well as pull atmospheric moisture from the \nsouthwest US. Cloud cover and precipitation over the next few days\nwill keep temperatures around normal, in the 80s to low 90s. An \nincrease in atmospheric moisture through Thursday along with \nmodel soundings indicate slow moving storms will increase the \npotential for heavy rain. A 10-20% chance of showers exists across\nthe area today with moderate chances for the higher terrain \nacross southern MT and northern WY. Short term ensembles generally\nhave the highest precipitation amounts over the Absaroka \nBeartooths today (mainly for this evening). The potential for \nheavy rain becomes more widespread by Wednesday, spreading across \nsouthern MT and northern WY. Moderate to high chances for at least\na half an inch in an hour exist for the mountains and foothills\nWednesday evening. Those recreating near any burn scars such as \nthe Robertson Draw, East Side or Elk should use caution during \nthese times and stay aware of changing conditions. As far as \nthunderstorms go, they look to remain weak and confined to the \nmountains and foothills the next couple days. Late Wednesday into \nThursday, instability is expected to spread onto the plains, \nincreasing thunderstorm and heavy rainfall potential. \n\nFriday and Saturday, elevated atmospheric moisture will retreat \nsouth and mid level heights will build, allowing for hot \ntemperatures in the upper 90s and low 100s to return. Low chances\nfor an afternoon shower and thunderstorms remain in the forecast\nfor these days. Ensembles hint at some energy moving across\nnorthern MT over the weekend which would increase the potential \nfor breezy conditions. With the increasing hot, dry and \npotentially breezy conditions, fire weather concerns could arise \nfor those who have not received recent precipitation. This will be\nsomething to watch. Temperatures in the 90s to 100 persist Sunday\nand Monday. TS",
